From 88a009f47c412977692dfd8195eab913bbdd5210 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: liuwang97 <17954538+liuwang97@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] touchpad: speed-dependent two-finger scroll gain (L410)

libinput scrolls touchpads at a constant 10.52 units per mm of finger
travel, whatever the speed, and the only upstream way to change that, a
custom acceleration profile, also replaces the adaptive pointer
acceleration and is not configurable in KWin 6.7 or Mutter.

Multiply the two-finger scroll delta in tp_filter_scroll() by G(v): v is
the finger speed in mm/s over the last 50 ms of the stroke (reset when a
scroll starts, after a 100 ms pause and on reversal), G a piecewise linear
curve read from /etc/l410/scroll-accel.conf when a stroke starts and the
file changed. The built-in default is ChromeOS's default touchpad scroll
curve (gestures library, Scroll Sensitivity 3) with its corners rounded:
gain 1 up to 45 mm/s, v/75 from 105 mm/s, peak 7.3x at 600 mm/s, output
speed never decreasing. Touchpads with a guessed resolution are left
alone; the threshold, direction lock and the terminating zero event of
evdev_post_scroll() are unchanged.

L410_SCROLL_ACCEL_CONFIG (secure_getenv, ignored by the compositor) points
tests at another curve. The library logs speed and gain per frame at debug
priority.
